      { term: 'Provenance', definition: 'The documented chain of ownership for a collectible, from creation to current owner. Complete provenance increases value by 20-50% and is essential for authentication at major auction houses.', example: 'A Monet painting with documentation tracing ownership through three museum collections and two private collectors has rock-solid provenance. One with a 20-year gap in ownership history raises forgery concerns.', sentence: 'The auction house refused to list the painting because its provenance had an unexplained 30-year gap, raising serious authentication concerns.' },
      { term: 'Fractional Ownership', definition: 'Splitting a high-value collectible into shares that multiple investors can purchase. SEC-regulated platforms file offerings and handle storage, insurance, and eventual sale.', example: 'Masterworks purchased a Basquiat painting for $10.5 million and offered shares to 2,000 investors. When the painting sold for $15.8 million two years later, each share holder received their proportional profit.', sentence: 'Through fractional ownership, she invested $500 in a Banksy painting that she could never have afforded on her own.' },
      { term: 'Authentication & Grading', definition: 'The process of verifying a collectible is genuine and rating its condition on a standardized scale. PSA grades cards 1-10, GIA certifies diamonds, and auction houses authenticate art.', example: 'A raw (ungraded) 1st edition Charizard Pokemon card might sell for $10,000. Once PSA-graded at 10 (gem mint), the same card is worth $420,000+.', sentence: 'He sent his vintage baseball card collection to PSA for authentication and grading, knowing that a PSA 10 grade could multiply the value tenfold.' },
      { term: 'Blue-Chip Art', definition: 'Works by established, auction-proven artists (Picasso, Warhol, Basquiat, Banksy, Monet) with consistent price appreciation. These artists\' works form the Artprice100 index.', example: 'Andy Warhol\'s "Shot Sage Blue Marilyn" sold for $195 million in 2022. Warhol\'s works have averaged 9.1% annual appreciation over 30 years.', sentence: 'Her art advisor recommended allocating to blue-chip art, established artists whose works have decades of auction data showing consistent appreciation.' },
      { term: 'Buyer\'s Premium', definition: 'An additional fee charged by auction houses on top of the hammer price, typically 15-25% of the sale price. Combined with seller commissions, total transaction costs can reach 30-40%.', example: 'A painting with a $1 million hammer price at Sotheby\'s actually costs the buyer $1.25 million after the 25% buyer\'s premium. If the seller also pays 10% commission, $350K goes to Sotheby\'s.', sentence: 'After factoring in the 20% buyer\'s premium and insurance costs, the total cost of acquiring the painting was 25% above the listed auction estimate.' },
      { term: 'Condition Premium', definition: 'The multiplier in value between a collectible in average condition versus mint/perfect condition. For rare items, the premium between grades can be 5-50x.', example: 'A 1952 Topps Mickey Mantle card in PSA 7 condition is worth ~$500,000. In PSA 10 (gem mint), it sold for $12.6 million, a 25x condition premium.', sentence: 'The condition premium between his PSA 9 and a PSA 10 Charizard card was over 8x, proving that in collectibles, condition is everything.' }
