      { takeaway: 'The SPAC boom and bust is a masterclass in structural conflicts', detail: 'The 20% sponsor promote created a $100M incentive to complete ANY deal, good or bad. Forward projection loopholes let pre-revenue companies present $5B revenue targets. Celebrity endorsements replaced fundamental analysis. Result: 60%+ of de-SPACed companies below $5 and $100B+ in investor losses. Always analyze who profits and whether incentives align with investor interests.' },
      { takeaway: 'Pre-merger SPAC arbitrage offered genuine asymmetric risk-reward', detail: 'Buying pre-merger SPACs near $10 trust value provided a downside floor with unlimited upside potential on popular merger announcements. Sophisticated investors earned consistent 8-15% returns using this strategy during the boom. The key: always redeem before bad mergers, never hold through a de-SPAC without rigorous fundamental analysis.' },
      { takeaway: 'SPACs are a structure, not an asset class, evaluate each deal on fundamentals', detail: 'DraftKings succeeded via SPAC because it was a real company with real revenue. Nikola failed because it was a pre-revenue fantasy. The SPAC structure didn\'t determine the outcome, the quality of the underlying company did. After the bust, surviving SPACs tend to have better targets because bubble-era excess has been washed out.' }
